1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,430
This text claims it's from the Postal Service, asking for a two-dollar redelivery fee.

2
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:12,190
Is it real? Imposter scams were nearly one in three fraud reports to the FTC in 2025,

3
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:15,870
with 3.5 billion dollars in reported losses.

4
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:19,230
When you get a text like this, how do you check it?

5
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:22,750
The standard advice is to contact the company yourself.

6
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:27,070
But if you paste a suspicious message into an AI chatbot,

7
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:29,550
you get a verdict with nothing you can check.

8
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:35,710
Scammers use AI too, so good grammar proves nothing. Meet Callback.

9
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:40,030
Instead of guessing, Callback runs a live investigation.

10
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:43,230
We paste the message and click Check it.

11
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:48,510
Callback works out who the message claims to be, here the US Postal Service,

12
00:00:48,560 --> 00:00:53,790
from a hand-checked list of official organizations, never from the message itself.

13
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:58,510
Then it compares the message's link with the real domain, usps.com.

14
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:04,000
The link isn't official, it contains the brand name, and the domain isn't registered.

15
00:01:04,050 --> 00:01:06,560
The verdict: doesn't match.

16
00:01:06,610 --> 00:01:10,080
Callback doesn't just cry scam on everything.

17
00:01:10,130 --> 00:01:14,400
When you get a real fraud alert from your bank, like Wells Fargo,

18
00:01:14,450 --> 00:01:19,520
it confirms the link is on wellsfargo.com and checks the phone number

19
00:01:19,570 --> 00:01:24,080
against Wells Fargo's own contact page. The verdict: it matches.

20
00:01:24,130 --> 00:01:29,760
That matters, because false alarms teach people to ignore real warnings.

21
00:01:29,810 --> 00:01:32,560
Callback also reads screenshots.

22
00:01:32,610 --> 00:01:38,160
Drop in an image of a suspicious text, and Gemini vision reads the message.

23
00:01:38,210 --> 00:01:43,040
Then a guard re-checks the result and drops anything the AI invented

24
00:01:43,090 --> 00:01:46,000
that isn't really in the image.

25
00:01:46,050 --> 00:01:51,280
If someone already clicked the link or replied, panic makes people freeze.

26
00:01:51,330 --> 00:01:57,200
Callback's incident panel gives a checklist for what to do next, based on how they paid,

27
00:01:57,250 --> 00:02:00,560
and downloads an incident summary with the evidence,

28
00:02:00,610 --> 00:02:04,570
ready to paste into a report for the FTC.

29
00:02:04,620 --> 00:02:10,170
Here is what makes Callback different: the AI never decides the verdict.

30
00:02:10,220 --> 00:02:14,970
Gemini only reads messy text and writes the plain-English explanation,

31
00:02:15,020 --> 00:02:21,370
and every sentence has to cite a numbered piece of evidence, like E1, or it gets dropped.

32
00:02:21,420 --> 00:02:26,490
Seven fixed rules decide whether the contact details match.

33
00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:31,850
We tested this on 35 synthetic messages that we made up for this project.

34
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:39,850
Gemini alone flagged all 20 scams, but it also called a real payment receipt a scam, and showed no evidence.

35
00:02:40,060 --> 00:02:45,290
Callback caught 15 of the 20 scams, with zero false alarms,

36
00:02:45,340 --> 00:02:49,450
backed 74 of 80 explanation sentences with evidence,

37
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:56,330
and abstained on 10 of the 35 messages instead of guessing.

38
00:02:56,380 --> 00:03:03,450
Callback is honest about its limits. It covers US numbers and 27 hand-checked organizations.

39
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:08,200
A phone number can only be confirmed where the organization publishes it.

40
00:03:08,250 --> 00:03:13,400
If an organization isn't on the list, or its site blocks automated checks,

41
00:03:13,450 --> 00:03:17,400
Callback says it can't verify, instead of guessing.

42
00:03:17,450 --> 00:03:20,360
I'm John Tewolde, and this is Callback.

43
00:03:20,410 --> 00:03:26,120
Try it live at callback-lac.vercel.app.

44
00:03:26,170 --> 00:03:31,240
Don't trust the number in the message. Callback finds the real one.
